  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "How does the bisection method handle the interval containing a root at each step?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Retain only the longer half",
    "B": "Randomly discard an endpoint",
    "C": "Split at the midpoint; stop if its function value is zero, otherwise retain the half whose endpoint function values have opposite signs",
    "D": "Retain both halves"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "For a continuous function on a bracket \\([a,b]\\) with \\(f(a)f(b)<0\\), evaluate the midpoint \\(m=(a+b)/2\\). If \\(f(m)=0\\), the root has been found and the method stops. Otherwise, retain \\([a,m]\\) if \\(f(a)f(m)<0\\), or \\([m,b]\\) if \\(f(m)f(b)<0\\). The intermediate value theorem guarantees a root in the retained half.",
